 ## Production helper lifecycle
 
-`roles/diaries/files/sync/scripts/` is reserved for permanent supported production operator tooling. Temporary feature-only migration, capture or verification helpers must be explicitly deployed for the feature and explicitly removed during feature close-out; they must not accumulate in the always-synchronized script tree. Where a deployed copy can survive source removal, the close-out must remove that exact remote helper and verify its absence.
+`roles/diaries/files/sync/scripts/` is reserved for permanent supported production operator tooling. The deployed `{{ diaries_project_dir }}/scripts/` directory is a **managed directory**: it is synchronized separately with `rsync --delete --delete-excluded`, while the permanent templated operator scripts are explicitly protected and then rendered by Ansible. Any other stale or feature-specific entry left in the deployed scripts directory is therefore removed automatically on the next normal copy deployment.
 
-The permanent `roles/diaries/tests/verify-production-deployment-contract.py` regression enforces the approved synchronized script set and rejects unclassified feature/step-shaped helpers. Completed temporary tooling should be preserved, when needed, with the corresponding Diaries change-control evidence rather than left in the role.
+Temporary feature-only migration, capture or verification helpers must be explicitly deployed for the feature and explicitly removed during feature close-out; they must not accumulate in the permanent synchronized source set. If a feature temporarily installs a helper directly on production, the next normal managed-script synchronization also provides a safety net by deleting it unless it has been deliberately classified as permanent source or a protected permanent template.
+
+The project-root synchronization deliberately excludes `scripts/`, so `--delete` is never applied to the wider Diaries project directory. The permanent `roles/diaries/tests/verify-production-deployment-contract.py` regression enforces this scope, the approved synchronized script set, the protected template set and the absence of unclassified feature/step-shaped helpers. Completed temporary tooling should be preserved, when needed, with the corresponding Diaries change-control evidence rather than left in the role.
 
 ## Backup and restore
 
--- a/roles/diaries/files/sync/scripts/README.md
+++ b/roles/diaries/files/sync/scripts/README.md
@@ -9,7 +9,9 @@
 
 This synchronized directory is a small allow-list of permanent production operator tooling. Adding a file here means declaring that command part of the supported ongoing production surface; `roles/diaries/tests/verify-production-deployment-contract.py` fails if the source set grows without an explicit supported classification.
 
-A **temporary feature-only production helper** must not be left indefinitely in this always-synchronized tree. When a feature genuinely needs one, it must be explicitly deployed for that feature and explicitly removed during feature close-out, with deployed absence verified if source removal alone cannot remove the remote copy. Historical source belongs with the completed change-control evidence rather than in this directory.
+The deployed production `scripts/` directory is managed separately from the project root. Its synchronize task uses `--delete` and `--delete-excluded`, so entries which are neither present in this static source directory nor explicitly protected permanent templates are removed from production. The protected files are the Ansible-rendered permanent commands (`start.sh`, `stop.sh`, `status.sh`, `logs.sh` and the `shell-prompt-*.sh` scripts); Ansible templates them after the managed static sync.
+
+A **temporary feature-only production helper** must not be left indefinitely in this always-synchronized tree. When a feature genuinely needs one, it must be explicitly deployed for that feature and explicitly removed during feature close-out. The managed-directory deletion is an additional safety net: a forgotten deployed helper is removed by the next normal copy deployment unless it has been deliberately promoted into the permanent managed set. Historical source belongs with the completed change-control evidence rather than in this directory.
 
 Feature/step-shaped names are additionally treated as suspicious by the permanent deployment-contract regression. A genuinely permanent command with such a name requires an exact documented exception as well as inclusion in the supported-script set.
 
